Build a database for managing students, fee types, invoices, payments, discounts, and outstanding balances. Include queries for unpaid fees, payment history, total collections, and overdue payments.

Repo structure to build toward
student-fees-database/
├── erd/
│   └── schema.png              # exported ERD image
├── sql/
│   ├── schema.sql               # CREATE TABLE statements
│   ├── seed.sql                 # sample data (INSERT statements)
│   └── queries.sql              # the 8+ required queries, commented
├── results/
│   └── query_results.md         # output of each query, pasted in
├── DATA_QUALITY.md              # the AI data-quality note
├── README.md
└── .gitignore
Step 1: Repo setup
powershell
mkdir student-fees-database
cd student-fees-database
git init
mkdir erd, sql, results

Install sqlite3 if it isn’t already available (check with sqlite3 --version in PowerShell; it comes bundled with Python’s standard library too, so python -c "import sqlite3; print(sqlite3.sqlite_version)" works as a fallback check). Install DB Browser for SQLite from the link above if you want a GUI.

.gitignore:

*.db-journal
__pycache__/

Note: we’ll likely commit the actual .db file, unlike .venv in your Python projects, since a small SQLite database file is the easiest way for a marker to open and inspect your work directly. We’ll decide this for sure in Step 7.

Commit:

powershell
git add .
git commit -m "Set up student fees database project"
Step 2: Design the domain and the schema

The domain brief (write 3-4 sentences of this into your README later): a school tracks students, the types of fees it charges (tuition, library, exam fees, etc.), invoices issued to students for those fees, discounts applied to specific invoices, and payments students make against those invoices. The system needs to answer: who owes money, who’s paid what, how much has been collected, and who’s overdue.

Entities and relationships:

A student can have many invoices.
A fee type can appear on many invoices (tuition is charged to every student, for instance).
An invoice can have zero or more discounts applied to it.
An invoice can have zero or more payments made against it (fees are often paid in installments).

This is a standard one-to-many chain in every direction, which keeps the schema in third normal form: no repeating groups, every non-key column depends only on its table’s primary key, and nothing is duplicated across rows (a student’s name lives in exactly one place, not copied onto every invoice).

Schema — type this into sql/schema.sql yourself rather than pasting, so you internalize it:

sql
CREATE TABLE students (
    student_id      INTEGER PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT,
    full_name       TEXT NOT NULL,
    email           TEXT NOT NULL UNIQUE,
    enrollment_date TEXT NOT NULL,
    status          TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT 'active'
);

CREATE TABLE fee_types (
    fee_type_id     INTEGER PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT,
    name            TEXT NOT NULL UNIQUE,
    description     TEXT,
    default_amount  NUMERIC NOT NULL CHECK (default_amount >= 0)
);

CREATE TABLE invoices (
    invoice_id      INTEGER PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT,
    student_id      INTEGER NOT NULL REFERENCES students(student_id),
    fee_type_id     INTEGER NOT NULL REFERENCES fee_types(fee_type_id),
    amount_due      NUMERIC NOT NULL CHECK (amount_due >= 0),
    issue_date      TEXT NOT NULL,
    due_date        TEXT NOT NULL,
    status          TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT 'open'
);

CREATE TABLE discounts (
    discount_id     INTEGER PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT,
    invoice_id      INTEGER NOT NULL REFERENCES invoices(invoice_id),
    description     TEXT NOT NULL,
    amount          NUMERIC NOT NULL CHECK (amount >= 0)
);

CREATE TABLE payments (
    payment_id      INTEGER PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT,
    invoice_id      INTEGER NOT NULL REFERENCES invoices(invoice_id),
    amount_paid     NUMERIC NOT NULL CHECK (amount_paid > 0),
    payment_date    TEXT NOT NULL,
    method          TEXT NOT NULL
);

-- Indexes: speed up the lookups the required queries will do most often
CREATE INDEX idx_invoices_student_id ON invoices(student_id);
CREATE INDEX idx_invoices_due_date   ON invoices(due_date);
CREATE INDEX idx_payments_invoice_id ON payments(invoice_id);
CREATE INDEX idx_discounts_invoice_id ON discounts(invoice_id);

Why indexes on those specific columns: every foreign key you’ll filter or join on (student_id, invoice_id) and the column your overdue query will filter by (due_date) benefit most, since without an index SQLite has to scan every row to find matches. Reference on SQLite’s indexing: https://www.sqlite.org/queryplanner.html

A design note worth writing into your README: outstanding balance isn’t stored as a column anywhere. It’s calculated as amount_due - discounts - payments for each invoice. Storing a redundant “balance” column that you’d have to keep in sync by hand is exactly the kind of duplication normalization avoids; a query derives it instead. This is a good sentence to include in your schema write-up, since it shows you understand why the design avoids redundancy, not just that it does.

Build the database:

powershell
sqlite3 fees.db < sql/schema.sql
Step 3: The ERD

Go to dbdiagram.io and describe the five tables and their foreign-key relationships using their DSL (it’s close to SQL and quick to pick up from their docs on the site). Export it as a PNG and save it to erd/schema.png.

The diagram should show the one-to-many relationships: students → invoices, fee_types → invoices, invoices → discounts, invoices → payments.

Step 4: Seed data

File: sql/seed.sql

Write at least:

5-8 students
3-5 fee types (tuition, library, lab, exam, late fee)
10-15 invoices spread across students and fee types, with a mix of due dates (some in the past, so you have real overdue cases to query)
A few discounts on some invoices
Enough payments that some invoices are fully paid, some partially paid, and some untouched, so your “unpaid” and “overdue” queries actually return varied results

Example row shape, so you can write the rest in the same pattern:

sql
INSERT INTO students (full_name, email, enrollment_date, status)
VALUES ('Ada Obi', 'ada.obi@example.com', '2026-01-15', 'active');

INSERT INTO fee_types (name, description, default_amount)
VALUES ('Tuition', 'Semester tuition fee', 150000);

INSERT INTO invoices (student_id, fee_type_id, amount_due, issue_date, due_date, status)
VALUES (1, 1, 150000, '2026-01-15', '2026-02-15', 'open');

INSERT INTO payments (invoice_id, amount_paid, payment_date, method)
VALUES (1, 50000, '2026-02-01', 'bank_transfer');

Load it:

powershell
sqlite3 fees.db < sql/seed.sql
Step 5: Write at least 8 SQL queries

File: sql/queries.sql. Comment each query with its purpose before the SQL. Here are the 8, with the first two written out as a pattern and the rest as specs for you to write:

1. Unpaid fees (invoices with an outstanding balance):

sql
-- 1. Invoices with an outstanding balance
SELECT
    i.invoice_id,
    s.full_name,
    ft.name AS fee_type,
    i.amount_due,
    COALESCE(SUM(p.amount_paid), 0) AS total_paid,
    COALESCE((SELECT SUM(d.amount) FROM discounts d WHERE d.invoice_id = i.invoice_id), 0) AS total_discount,
    i.amount_due - COALESCE((SELECT SUM(d.amount) FROM discounts d WHERE d.invoice_id = i.invoice_id), 0)
        - COALESCE(SUM(p.amount_paid), 0) AS balance
FROM invoices i
JOIN students s  ON s.student_id = i.student_id
JOIN fee_types ft ON ft.fee_type_id = i.fee_type_id
LEFT JOIN payments p ON p.invoice_id = i.invoice_id
GROUP BY i.invoice_id
HAVING balance > 0;

2. Payment history for a specific student:

sql
-- 2. Payment history for a given student
SELECT s.full_name, i.invoice_id, p.payment_date, p.amount_paid, p.method
FROM payments p
JOIN invoices i ON i.invoice_id = p.invoice_id
JOIN students s ON s.student_id = i.student_id
WHERE s.student_id = 1
ORDER BY p.payment_date;

3. Total collections (overall, and by fee type): SUM(amount_paid) from payments, grouped by joining through to fee_types for the by-type version. Two separate queries, or one with GROUP BY ft.name.

4. Overdue payments: invoices where due_date < date('now') and the calculated balance (same subquery pattern as query 1) is still greater than 0. SQLite’s date functions: https://www.sqlite.org/lang_datefunc.html

5. Outstanding balance per student (not per invoice): same balance calculation as query 1, but GROUP BY s.student_id instead of per-invoice, summing across all of a student’s invoices.

6. Students with no payments at all: a LEFT JOIN from students through invoices to payments, filtering WHERE p.payment_id IS NULL. This is a good one to discuss in your write-up as a classic outer-join use case.

7. Total discounts given, per fee type or overall: SUM(amount) from discounts, optionally joined through invoices to fee_types for a breakdown.

8. A monthly collections trend: SUM(amount_paid) from payments grouped by strftime('%Y-%m', payment_date), so you can see collections by month. This one demonstrates date functions and GROUP BY together.

Run each with:

powershell
sqlite3 fees.db < sql/queries.sql

Or run them one at a time in DB Browser for SQLite if you want to see formatted result tables more easily, since the command line prints plain text.

Step 6: Capture query results

File: results/query_results.md. For each of the 8 queries, paste in: the query’s purpose (one line), and the actual output rows you got when you ran it against your seed data. This is what satisfies “Query results” in the submission list, a marker shouldn’t have to run your SQL themselves to see what it produces.

Step 7: Decide whether to commit the .db file

Simplest and recommended: commit fees.db itself, so anyone cloning the repo can open it immediately in DB Browser for SQLite without running any setup scripts. Add this to your README setup section: “The database is included as fees.db; alternatively, rebuild it from scratch with sqlite3 fees.db < sql/schema.sql then < sql/seed.sql.” Giving both options costs nothing and covers whoever’s grading it.

Step 8: The AI data-quality note

File: DATA_QUALITY.md. This connects your schema work to the broader point the activity description makes (“explain how data quality affects downstream AI systems”). Write 300-500 words covering:

Garbage in, garbage out: an AI model trained on or querying this data (say, a chatbot answering “has this student paid?” or a model predicting which students are at risk of dropping out due to unpaid fees) is only as reliable as the underlying data. A missing payment record, a wrongly entered amount_due, or a duplicate invoice would directly corrupt that output.
Specific risks in this schema: what happens to a prediction model if email isn’t actually unique in practice (duplicate student records), if payment_date is entered inconsistently (some systems logging the transaction date, others the date it was reconciled), or if discounts are applied inconsistently without a clear audit trail.
How constraints help: your CHECK constraints, NOT NULL constraints, and foreign keys aren’t just SQL formality, they’re a first line of defense against the kind of bad data that quietly poisons downstream analytics or AI systems, by rejecting invalid rows before they ever get written.
What this schema still doesn’t guarantee: constraints catch structural problems (a negative payment, an invoice pointing to a student that doesn’t exist), but not semantic ones (a correct-looking but wrong amount_due, a payment logged against the wrong invoice). Worth a sentence acknowledging that data quality is bigger than schema design alone.
Step 9: README
markdown
# Student Fees Database

A relational database for managing students, fee types, invoices, payments,
discounts, and outstanding balances, with SQL queries to answer common
billing questions.

## Domain

[Your 3-4 sentence domain brief from Step 2]

## Setup

Requires SQLite (`sqlite3` command, or DB Browser for SQLite as a GUI client).

The database is included as `fees.db`. To rebuild it from scratch instead:

```bash
sqlite3 fees.db < sql/schema.sql
sqlite3 fees.db < sql/seed.sql
```

## Running the queries

```bash
sqlite3 fees.db < sql/queries.sql
```

Or open `fees.db` in DB Browser for SQLite and run individual queries from
`sql/queries.sql` under the "Execute SQL" tab.

## Files

| Path | Contents |
|---|---|
| `erd/schema.png` | Entity-relationship diagram |
| `sql/schema.sql` | Table definitions, keys, constraints, indexes |
| `sql/seed.sql` | Sample student, invoice, payment, and discount data |
| `sql/queries.sql` | 8 queries answering common billing questions |
| `results/query_results.md` | Output of each query against the seed data |
| `DATA_QUALITY.md` | Note on how data quality affects downstream AI systems |

## Schema design notes

[2-3 sentences: why these 5 tables, why outstanding balance is calculated
rather than stored, why these specific indexes]
